		<item>
		<title>Russia claims 44 drones shot down near Moscow in one night — and the attack isn&#8217;t over yet</title>
		<link>https://euromaidanpress.com/2025/09/23/russia-claims-44-drones-shot-down-near-moscow-in-one-night-and-the-attack-isnt-over-yet/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Yuri Zoria]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 23 Sep 2025 10:14:40 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Latest news Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[deep strikes]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[drone strikes]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[main]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Moscow]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://euromaidanpress.com/?p=361621</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[From 19:30 on 22 September to 12:25 on 23 September, Moscow mayor Sobyanin has posted ongoing interception reports and claimed the attack was still ongoing.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><img width="662" height="677" src="https://euromaidanpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/5375069498870200280.jpg" class="attachment-large size-large wp-post-image" alt="russia claims 44 drones shot down near moscow one night — attack still isn&#039;t over yet trace russian surface-to-air missile oblast 23 2025 telegram/exilenova+ 5375069498870200280 local telegram channels posted videos" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://euromaidanpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/5375069498870200280.jpg 662w, https://euromaidanpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/5375069498870200280-293x300.jpg 293w, https://euromaidanpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/5375069498870200280-380x389.jpg 380w, https://euromaidanpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/5375069498870200280-45x45.jpg 45w" sizes="(max-width: 662px) 100vw, 662px" /></p><div data-type="_mgwidget" data-widget-id="1845881"></div>        
        <script>
            (function(w,q){w[q]=w[q]||[];w[q].push(["_mgc.load"])})(window,"_mgq");
        </script>
    <p data-start="252" data-end="568">Local Telegram channels posted videos of explosions in and around Russia’s capital, Moscow, as drone attacks reportedly began in the evening of 22 September. According to Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin, Russia’s air defense have remained active for over 15 hours, allegedly downing 43 drones by 12:25 p.m. on 23 September. Currently, it is unclear if the attack has caused any damage.</p>
<div class="pk-content-block pk-block-bg pk-block-bg-light" data-start="252" data-end="568">Ukraine continues its strategic campaign of deep strikes on Russian military targets, both within Russia and in occupied territories. In recent months, these operations have focused on oil production and transport infrastructure, aiming to disrupt Russian military logistics and reduce export revenues. The latest attack on Moscow — the most heavily defended city in Russia — may be intended to force the Kremlin to divert additional air defense systems to the capital, thereby exposing more vulnerable targets elsewhere to future Ukrainian strikes.</div>
<h3 id="moscow-under-drone-attack-since-22-september-evening" data-start="575" data-end="707">Moscow under drone attack since 22 September evening</h3>
<p data-start="709" data-end="1192">Authorities in Moscow say the capital came under sustained drone attack beginning in the evening of 22 September, with the city’s air defenses remaining active throughout the night and into 23 September.</p><div data-fuse="incontent_1"></div>
<p data-start="709" data-end="1192">The <a href="https://t.me/mos_sobyanin/16796">first public message</a> from Mayor Sergei Sobyanin came at 19:30 on Saturday, announcing that emergency services were responding as drones approached Moscow. <strong>He did not confirm any hits, but said air defense had intercepted UAVs and that specialists were working at impact sites.</strong></p>
<p data-start="1194" data-end="1699">Over the next 15 hours, Sobyanin posted <strong data-start="1234" data-end="1257">19 separate updates</strong>, each claiming that additional drones had been destroyed.</p>
<div class="pk-content-block pk-block-bg pk-block-bg-light" data-start="176" data-end="337">By 12:25 on 23 September, <strong data-start="202" data-end="258">Euromaidan Press’ count based on Sobyanin’s individual updates indicates that 44 drones had allegedly been downed</strong> during the extended attack. The Russian mayor claimed in his <a href="https://t.me/mos_sobyanin/16815">latest update</a> that <em>“The interception of enemy drones continues.”</em> Sobyanin never mentioned any damage or injuries. </div>
<p data-start="1194" data-end="1699">Ukrainian and Russian Telegram channels published videos featuring the sound of air defense-linked explosions and drones flying over several locations in Moscow Oblast.</p>


<blockquote class="twitter-tweet"><a href="https://twitter.com/EuromaidanPress/status/1970425468806447587"></a></blockquote><script async src="https://platform.twitter.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"></script>



<h3 id="russian-mods-claims" data-start="1194" data-end="1699">Russian MoD’s claims</h3><div data-fuse="incontent_2"></div>
<p data-start="1701" data-end="2117">Meanwhile, the Russian Defense Ministry claimed that air defense systems had intercepted 69 drones across Belgorod, Bryansk, Kaluga, Kursk, Rostov, Ryazan, Samara, Saratov oblasts, the Moscow region, and Russian-occupied Crimea. </p>
<h3 id="airport-shutdowns" data-start="1701" data-end="2117">Airport shutdowns</h3>
<p data-start="2119" data-end="2539">Following the start of the attacks, Sheremetyevo Airport in Moscow enacted its emergency “Carpet” protocol. Flights were rerouted to other cities and airports. Russian outlet Baza also reported a full closure of the airspace above Moscow. In addition to Sheremetyevo, airports in Kazan, Nizhnekamsk, and Gelendzhik imposed temporary flight restrictions due to the drone threat, according to Rosaviatsiya.</p>

